15
00:01:26,894 --> 00:01:36,324
So if we actually try to define what graph engineering is, I think a good definition is it's just the practice of designing agent work as an explicit graph.

16
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:42,160
So where nodes do work and edges effectively route state between them.

17
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:46,960
And I tend to think of it in terms of three different types of graphs.

18
00:01:47,280 --> 00:01:52,400
We have our general execution graphs, which you're probably very familiar with.

19
00:01:52,526 --> 00:02:00,240
So these are the workflow graphs with routers and agents as actual nodes that happen with inside of an agent harness.

20
00:02:00,686 --> 00:02:05,440
But there's other ways that we can think about graph engineering too that overlap with this.

21
00:02:05,678 --> 00:02:07,940
So that includes context graphs.

22
00:02:08,080 --> 00:02:14,788
These are decision traces, instances of what's happened, and how it connects to other data inside of your domain.

23
00:02:15,120 --> 00:02:18,852
And then there's also something that I'd call more procedural graphs.

24
00:02:19,182 --> 00:02:33,040
So this is almost like having a skill or procedural knowledge as a graph, where the steps might not be actual agents, but they might be steps inside of a way of doing something, basically, a procedure.

25
00:02:33,120 --> 00:02:42,320
So different calls, maybe a step for a decision model, steps for different agents, whether to escalate, validation steps, and these sorts of things.

26
00:02:42,670 --> 00:02:47,600
And I'm really going to focus on these last two because I think we don't talk about them enough.

27
00:02:47,854 --> 00:02:56,324
And I think by understanding these, you can actually get a little bit at what the first graph is trying to do as well, but in a very portable and efficient way.

28
00:02:57,678 --> 00:03:04,356
So graph engineering lets agents really take on bigger, more complex work, and that's why we care about it.

29
00:03:04,718 --> 00:03:13,280
And really, if you were to split it up, I think in terms of the benefits that a graph offers agents in this context, it would be predictability.

30
00:03:13,486 --> 00:03:31,812
So being able to constrain a path that an agent takes, making sure that the answer is basically accurate from a task that it's doing, and having an explicit graph, the ability to govern, control, and guide what the agent does and audit it so you can understand what it's doing and make it better next time.

31
00:03:32,174 --> 00:03:38,276
And then the third one, which is going to be interesting, that we'll talk about is how do we actually make graph engineering portable?

32
00:03:38,638 --> 00:03:53,252
And instead of thinking about a graph as something that happens in the runtime, it could be something that you can store and actually move between different run times and have inside of a knowledge layer, which gives you more interoperability and allows you to have a very simple stack.

33
00:03:54,496 --> 00:03:58,656
And in the runtime, when we think about the graph, it's really like a piece of orchestration.

34
00:03:58,736 --> 00:04:14,340
So we're probably all of us or a large portion of us are probably familiar with Lang Graph, ADK, and all these other agent frameworks, even to a certain extent, anthropics, multi-agent research, and some other stuff fits inside of graph engineering.

